thrm{q} \\wedge \\mathrm{r})) \\\\\n& \\equiv \\mathrm{p} \\vee[(\\sim \\mathrm{q} \\vee \\sim \\mathrm{r}) \\wedge(\\mathrm{q} \\wedge \\mathrm{r})]\n\\end{aligned}$\n...[Distributive law]\n$\\equiv p \\vee[\\sim(q \\wedge r) \\wedge(q \\wedge r)]$\n...[De Morgan's law]\n$\\equiv \\mathrm{p} \\vee \\mathrm{F}$\n...[Complement law]\n$\\equiv \\mathrm{p}$\n[Identity law]","difficulty":"Hard","source":"MHT CET 2023 (10 May Shift 1)","_marksId":"65a261df0279619d9d8a3005"},{"id":64127,"subject":"Mathematics","chapter":"Sets and Relations","exam":"Engineering","examName":"MHT CET","q":"Let $\\mathrm{A}=[\\mathrm{a}, \\mathrm{b}, \\mathrm{c}, \\mathrm{d}], \\mathrm{B}=[1,2,3]$. Relation $\\mathrm{R}_1, \\mathrm{R}_2, \\mathrm{R}_4$ are as follows:\n\n\\begin{aligned}\n& \\mathrm{R}_1=[(\\mathrm{a}, 1),(\\mathrm{b}, 2),(\\mathrm{c}, 1),(\\mathrm{d}, 2)] \\\\\n& \\mathrm{R}_2=[(\\mathrm{a}, 1),(\\mathrm{b}, 1),(\\mathrm{c}, 1),(\\mathrm{d}, 1)] \\\\\n& \\mathrm{R}_3=[(\\mathrm{a}, 2),(\\mathrm{b}, 3),(\\mathrm{c}, 2),(\\mathrm{d}, 2)] \\\\\n& \\mathrm{R}_4=[(\\mathrm{a}, 1),(\\mathrm{b}, 2),(\\mathrm{a}, 2),(\\mathrm{d}, 3)], \\text { then }\n\\end{aligned}\n","options":["only $\\mathrm{R}_3$ and $\\mathrm{R}_4$ are not functions","only $R_1$ and $R_2$ are not functions","only $\\mathrm{R}_3$ is not a function","only $\\mathrm{R}_4$ is not a function"],"answer":3,"solution":"We find that in $R_4, f(a)=1$ and $f(a)=2$. Hence $R_4$ is not a function.","difficulty":"Easy","source":"MHT CET 2021 (20 Sep Shift 2)","_marksId":"65a260450279619d9d8a2325"},{"id":64128,"subject":"Mathematics","chapter":"Sets and Relations","exam":"Engineering","examName":"MHT CET","q":"$\\begin{array}{l}\n\\text { If } \\mathrm{A} \\text { and } \\mathrm{B} \\text { are subsets of universal set } \\mathrm{X} \\text { such that } \\mathrm{n}(\\mathrm{X})=200, \\mathrm{n}(\\mathrm{A})=90, \\mathrm{n}(\\mathrm{B})=80 \\text { , }\\\\\nn\\left(A^{\\prime} \\cap B^{\\prime}\\right)=40, \\text { then } n\\left(A \\cap B^{\\prime}\\right)=\n\\end{array}$","options":["70","80","20","10"],"answer":1,"solution":"<img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fmht_cet%252FeKKs_-GVd2utn24_S_6Rnp9JG-GWNWdU8B5OewFrcdk.original.fullsize.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2\" data-qx-orig-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fmht_cet%2FeKKs_-GVd2utn24_S_6Rnp9JG-GWNWdU8B5OewFrcdk.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\" data-qx-storage-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fmht_cet%2FeKKs_-GVd2utn24_S_6Rnp9JG-GWNWdU8B5OewFrcdk.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\"><br>\n\n\\begin{aligned}\nn\\left(A \\cap B^{\\prime}\\right) &=n(A)-n(A \\cap B) \\\\\nn(x) &=200 \\\\\n& \\sim(A \\cap B)=n(A)+n(B)+2\\left(A^{\\prime} \\cap B^{\\prime}\\right) \\\\\n&-n(X) \\\\\nn\\left(A \\cap B^{\\prime}\\right) &=90-10 \\\\\n&=80\n\\end{aligned}\n","difficulty":"Easy","source":"MHT CET 2020 (20 Oct Shift 2)","_marksId":"65a2601a0279619d9d8a21c6"},{"id":64129,"subject":"Mathematics","chapter":"Sets and Relations","exam":"Engineering","examName":"MHT CET","q":"If $\\mathrm{A}=\\{x, y, z\\}, \\mathrm{B}=\\{1,2\\}$, then the total number of relations from set $\\mathrm{A}$ to set $\\mathrm{B}$ are","options":["64","16","32","8"],"answer":0,"solution":"It is given that $A=\\{x, y, z\\}$ and $B=\\{1,2\\} .\\therefore A \\times B=\\{(x, 1),(x, 2),(y, 1),(y, 2),(z, 1),(z, 2)\\}$\nSince $n(A \\times B)=6$, the number of subsets of $A \\times B$ is $2^{6} .$\nTherefore, the number of relations from $A$ to $B$ is $2^{6} .$","difficulty":"Easy","source":"MHT CET 2020 (16 Oct Shift 1)","_marksId":"65a25fc30279619d9d8a1f09"},{"id":64130,"subject":"Mathematics","chapter":"Sets and Relations","exam":"Engineering","examName":"MHT CET","q":"Given $\\mathrm{A}=\\{1,2,3,4,5\\}, \\mathrm{B}=\\{1,4,5\\} .$ If $\\mathrm{R}$ is a relation from $\\mathrm{A}$ to $\\mathrm{B}$ such that $\\quad(x, y)\\in \\mathrm{R}$ with $x\\gt y$, then range of $\\mathrm{R}$ is","options":["{1,4}","{4,5}","{1,4,5}","{2,4}"],"answer":0,"solution":"$A=\\{1,2,3,4,5\\} ; B=\\{1,4,5\\}R: \\quad A \\rightarrow B(x\\gt y)$\nDomain \n$\\left(\\begin{array}{l}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 5\\end{array}\\right)$\nRange\n$\\left(\\begin{array}{l}1 \\\\ 4\\end{array}\\right)$\nRange $R=\\{1,4\\}$","difficulty":"Easy","source":"MHT CET 2020 (14 Oct Shift 2)","_marksId":"65a25f840279619d9d8a1d1a"},{"id":64131,"subject":"Mathematics","chapter":"Sets and Relations","exam":"Engineering","examName":"MHT CET","q":"If $A=\\{x / x$ is a prime number, $0 \\leq x \\leq 9]$, then the number of elements of power\nset of $A$ is","options":["12","4","16","8"],"answer":2,"solution":"$\\begin{aligned} A=\\{2,3,5,7\\} & \\\\ \\text { Power set of } A &=2^{4} \\\\ &=16 . \\end{aligned}$","difficulty":"Easy","source":"MHT CET 2020 (13 Oct Shift 2)","_marksId":"65a25f600279619d9d8a1bf8"},{"id":64132,"subject":"Mathematics","chapter":"Sets and Relations","exam":"Engineering","examName":"MHT CET","q":"If $A=\\{2,4\\}, \\quad B=\\{3,4,5\\}$, then $(\\mathrm{A} \\cap \\mathrm{B}) \\times(\\mathrm{AUB})=$","options":["$\\{(3,2),(3,4),(4,4),(5,4)\\}$","$\\{(2,3),(2,4),(2,5)\\}$","$\\{(4,2),(4,3),(4,4),(4,5)\\}$","$\\{(4,3),(4,4),(4,5)\\}$"],"answer":2,"solution":"$\\mathrm{A} \\cap \\mathrm{B}=\\{4\\}\\mathrm{A} \\cup \\mathrm{B}=\\{2,3,4,5\\}$\nHence\n$(\\mathrm{A} \\cap \\mathrm{B}) \\times(\\mathrm{A} \\cup \\mathrm{B})=\\{(4,2),(4,3),(4,4),(4,5)\\}$","difficulty":"Easy","source":"MHT CET 2020 (12 Oct Shift 1)","_marksId":"65a25f290279619d9d8a1a3c"},{"id":64133,"subject":"Mathematics","chapter":"Sets and Relations","exam":"Engineering","examName":"MHT CET","q":"If <math><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mfenced close=\"}\" open=\"{\" separators=\"|\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>R</mi><mo>:</mo><msu