</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo> <mrow> <msup> <mn>3</mn> <mn>2</mn> </msup> <mrow><mo>(</mo> <mrow> <mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow> <mo>)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo> <mrow> <mfrac> <mn>1</mn> <mrow> <mn>2</mn><mo>!</mo></mrow> </mfrac> <mo>+</mo><mfrac> <mrow> <mn>3</mn><mrow><mo>(</mo> <mrow> <mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow> <mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow> <mn>6</mn> </mfrac> <mo>+</mo><mn>.....</mn></mrow> <mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow> <mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow> </math><br /><math> <mrow> <mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo> <mrow> <mn>9</mn><mrow><mo>(</mo> <mrow> <mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow> <mo>)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo> <mrow> <mfrac> <mn>1</mn> <mrow> <mn>2</mn><mo>!</mo></mrow> </mfrac> <mo>+</mo><mn>......</mn></mrow> <mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow> <mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow> </math><br /><math> <mrow> <mi>Y</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo> <mrow> <mn>9</mn><mrow><mo>(</mo> <mrow> <mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow> <mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>:</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow> <mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow> </math><br /><math> <mrow> <mo>∴</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow> </math><br /><math> <mrow> <mo>∴</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>∩</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow> </math><br />(or)<br />Put <math> <mrow> <mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>.....</mn></mrow> </math> in X and Y<br /><math> <mrow> <mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo> <mrow> <mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>9</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>54</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>....</mn></mrow> <mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow> </math><br /><math> <mrow> <mi>Y</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo> <mrow> <mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>9</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>18</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>27</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>36</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>45</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>54</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>63......</mn></mrow> <mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow> </math><br /><math> <mrow> <mi>X</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow> </math><br /><math> <semantics> <mrow> <mo>∴</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>∩</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow> </semantics></math>","difficulty":"Hard","source":"MHT CET 2018","_marksId":"65a25edb0279619d9d8a17d8"},{"id":64136,"subject":"Mathematics","chapter":"Sets and Relations","exam":"Engineering","examName":"MHT CET","q":"The chances to fail in Physics are $20 \\%$ and the chances to fail in Mathematics are $10 \\% .$ What are the chances to fail in at least one subject?","options":["$28 \\%$","$38 \\%$","$72 \\%$","$82 \\%$"],"answer":0,"solution":"Let $P(A)=\\frac{20}{100}=\\frac{1}{5}, P(B)=\\frac{10}{100}=\\frac{1}{10}$\nSince, event are independent and we have to\n\n\\text { find } \\begin{aligned}\nP(A&\\cup B)=P(A)+P(B)-P(A) \\cdot P(B) \\\\\n&=\\frac{1}{5}+\\frac{1}{10}-\\frac{1}{5} \\cdot \\frac{1}{10} \\\\\n&=\\frac{3}{10}-\\frac{1}{50}=\\frac{14}{50}=\\frac{14}{50} \\times 100=28 \\%\n\\end{aligned}\n","difficulty":"Medium","source":"MHT CET 2008","_marksId":"65a25e5b0279619d9d8a13c3"}]}